Ablations on Dataset B (sigma 0.1, M=16, efC=100) over a 5x5 data-seed by build-seed matrix show that the recall gap came from the original construction defaults: each new node selected M0 neighbors and back-filled candidates rejected by the neighbor-selection heuristic (new_node_degree="M0", keep_pruned=true). Switching to reference-style semantics (new_node_degree="M", keep_pruned=false) raised mean recall@10 at ef=200 from {legacy_recall_mean} to {cured_recall_mean}, reduced the mean number of nodes that fail 1-NN self-retrieval from {legacy_unreach_mean} to {cured_unreach_mean}, and reduced Dataset B build time from {legacy_build_s} s to {cured_build_s} s within the same ablation run.

Protecting each node's last incoming edge reduced BFS-unreachable nodes to {protect_bfs_unreach} without improving recall ({protect_recall}), so pure in-edge starvation does not explain the gap. Leading hypothesis, not isolated by a direct test: back-filled intra-cluster links crowd out long-range links and reduce navigability. About {cured_unreach_mean} nodes per index still fail self-retrieval, so target T5 remains a MISS.
